The Double Choo-Toku Experiment aims to investigate the influence of active-sterile neutrinos on the disappearance of muons and other particles. The goal is to establish a limit on the active-sterile neutrino coupling, which could be as small as $10^{-18}$ GeV$^{-2}$ at 1 TeV energy. This study explores the feasibility of determining the coupling between e and $\mu$ leptons in the Double Choo-Toku Experiment. The calculation involves the interaction cross-sections of $\mu^-$, $\mu^+$, and $e^-$ with $ ar{
u}_\mu$ and $ ar{
u}_e$ and assumes that the neutrino background is composed of $ ar{
u}_\mu$ and $ ar{
u}_e$. The results indicate that the experiment can detect a difference in the interaction cross-sections of up to 10% due to active-sterile neutrino coupling, enabling the investigation of the coupling parameter as small as $10^{-19}$ GeV$^{-2}$. This analysis also examines the influence of $
u_\mu$ and $
u_e$ on the disappearance of $\mu^-$ and $e^-$, respectively. The study concludes that the disappearance of $\mu^-$ and $e^-$ can be explained by the mixing between active and sterile neutrinos, provided that the coupling parameter is above $10^{-19}$ GeV$^{-2}$.
